---
// "How scores are calculated": mirrors backend/app/api/v1/ews.py (run_assessment),
// core/rules/* and core/models/*. Numbers come from
// core/models/saved/warning_score_model.metrics.json; update them after retraining.
interface Props {
  open?: boolean;
}
const { open = false } = Astro.props;

const bands = [
  { cls: "badge-low", name: "Low", range: "< 20", lift: "under 1.15× baseline", share: "71.3%", observed: "5.4%" },
  { cls: "badge-medium", name: "Medium", range: "20–45", lift: "1.15–1.35×", share: "7.8%", observed: "10.3%" },
  { cls: "badge-high", name: "High", range: "45–70", lift: "1.35–1.65×", share: "7.8%", observed: "11.9%" },
  { cls: "badge-critical", name: "Critical", range: "≥ 70", lift: "over 1.65×", share: "13.1%", observed: "18.9%" },
];

const models = [
  {
    name: "Warning-score model",
    status: "In use: 75% of the final score",
    role: "Predicts the probability that the customer defaults. This is the main score.",
    algorithm: "XGBoost classifier (xgboost, hist): 430 trees, depth 5, learning rate 0.03. No class re-weighting, so the probabilities stay calibrated",
    data: "Home Credit Default Risk (Kaggle) only: all 307,511 customers. The data was converted to the CredX format first: 11.6M payment rows became 10.95M instalments, and 1.55M bureau and card records became credit facilities. Split 70 / 15 / 15 into train / validation / test",
    features: "19 features, computed by the same code in training and in production: late-payment share overall and in the last 6 instalments, payments over 30 days late, worst and average days late, lateness trend, late streak, recovery rate, partial payments, shortfall, history length, loan count, active loans, utilisation, card/overdraft utilisation, debt-to-income, payment-to-income, instalment-to-income",
    quality: "Test AUC 0.678 (Gini 0.355) on 46,127 held-out customers. Calibrated: in the riskiest 10%, the predicted default rate was 20.0% and the observed rate 20.9%",
    file: "core/models/saved/warning_score_model.joblib",
  },
  {
    name: "Cash-flow deterioration model",
    status: "In use: 25% of the final score",
    role: "Probability that monthly inflow is in sustained decline. Home Credit has no cash-flow data, so the main model cannot learn this signal and it is added separately.",
    algorithm: "Gradient Boosting Classifier (scikit-learn): 200 trees, depth 5, learning rate 0.1",
    data: "Synthetic 12-month cash-flow series generated for all 307,511 Home Credit applicants (3.69M monthly records) from their income, instalment and default label. Split 80 / 20 train / test. Not yet validated on real bank data",
    features: "12 trend features: 3- and 6-month slope, volatility, % change, min/mean ratio, share of declining months, current vs peak, recent average inflow, consistency",
    quality: "Test AUC 0.888 on 61,503 held-out synthetic series. Needs at least 3 months of cash flow",
    file: "core/models/saved/cashflow_model.joblib",
  },
  {
    name: "AI explanation",
    status: "In use",
    role: "Writes the plain-English explanation. It explains the score but never changes it.",
    algorithm: "DeepSeek V4 Flash (deepseek-ai/DeepSeek-V4-Flash-0731) via Nebius Token Factory; JSON output, thinking off",
    data: "Not trained on your data. Each request sends only the stored scores, signals and repayment facts for one customer",
    features: "",
    quality: "If the model is unavailable, a rule-based explanation is shown and labelled as such",
    file: "backend/app/services/explainability.py",
  },
];
---

<details class="method" open={open}>
  <summary>
    <span class="method-icon" aria-hidden="true">i</span>
    How scores are calculated
  </summary>

  <div class="method-body">
    <section>
      <h4>Warning score (0–100)</h4>
      <p class="method-formula">
        Final = 0.75 × Model score + 0.25 × Cash-flow score
      </p>
      <p>
        <strong>Model score:</strong> the warning-score model predicts this customer's
        probability of default. That probability is divided by the Home Credit baseline (8.07%)
        to give a multiple (lift), and the lift is placed on the same bands
        <code>core/rules/rules.md</code> uses. If the customer has less than 3 months of cash flow,
        the final score is the model score alone. If the model files are missing (for example on
        the Docker image), the score falls back to the rule average
        0.30 × Delay + 0.20 × Miss + 0.25 × Exposure + 0.25 × Cash flow.
      </p>
      <table class="method-table">
        <thead><tr><th>Band</th><th>Score</th><th>Predicted risk</th><th>Share of test customers</th><th>Actual default rate</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          {bands.map((b) => (
            <tr>
              <td><span class={`badge ${b.cls}`}>{b.name}</span></td>
              <td>{b.range}</td><td>{b.lift}</td><td>{b.share}</td><td>{b.observed}</td>
            </tr>
          ))}
        </tbody>
      </table>
      <p class="method-note">
        The bands are checked on 46,127 Home Credit customers the model never saw. The higher the
        band, the higher the share who actually defaulted. The baseline is 8.07%.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h4>Key terms</h4>
      <dl class="method-terms">
        <dt>Rule</dt>
        <dd>A fixed, written-down check, for example "more than 30% of payments late = 30 points".
          Anyone can read and verify it, and it never changes by itself. CredX has four rules.</dd>
        <dt>Model</dt>
        <dd>A formula learned from examples of past customers and whether they defaulted. Nobody
          writes its logic by hand; it finds the patterns in the data. CredX has two models.</dd>
        <dt>Score</dt>
        <dd>A number from 0 to 100. For every score except data quality, higher means more risk.</dd>
        <dt>Default</dt>
        <dd>What the model predicts. In the Home Credit data, a customer "defaulted" if they paid
          late by more than a set number of days on at least one of the first instalments of their loan.</dd>
        <dt>Instalment</dt>
        <dd>One scheduled payment on a loan (for example the 5 March EMI).</dd>
        <dt>Days past due (DPD)</dt>
        <dd>How many days after the due date an instalment was paid. 0 = on time.</dd>
        <dt>Late payment</dt>
        <dd>An instalment paid after its due date (DPD above 0).</dd>
        <dt>Miss</dt>
        <dd>Any instalment not paid <em>in full and on time</em>: paid late, not paid at all, or
          paid only partly.</dd>
        <dt>Streak</dt>
        <dd>Misses in a row, with no good payment in between.</dd>
        <dt>Recovery</dt>
        <dd>A miss that is followed by the next instalment paid on time and in full (at least 95%).
          It shows the customer got back on track.</dd>
        <dt>Exposure</dt>
        <dd>How much credit the customer owes or can draw on, across all their loans and cards.</dd>
        <dt>Utilisation</dt>
        <dd>Amount owed ÷ amount sanctioned (or ÷ the card limit). 90% means almost all of the
          credit has been used.</dd>
        <dt>Debt-to-income, payment-to-income</dt>
        <dd>Total amount owed ÷ monthly income, and monthly instalments ÷ monthly income. They
          show whether the customer can afford their debt. Income is the monthly cash-flow inflow.</dd>
        <dt>Cash flow</dt>
        <dd>Money coming into the customer's account each month (inflow).</dd>
        <dt>Baseline, lift</dt>
        <dd>The baseline is the average default rate, 8.07% in Home Credit. Lift is the
          customer's predicted default rate ÷ the baseline: 2× means twice as risky as average.</dd>
        <dt>Thin file</dt>
        <dd>A customer with too little history to judge, shown as Insufficient data.</dd>
      </dl>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h4>Rule scores <small>shown to explain the signals, not added to the final score</small></h4>
      <p>
        The model reads the underlying repayment, exposure and income data directly. The four
        rules turn the same data into readable 0–100 scores and warning messages. In each rule,
        the <strong>cutoffs</strong> (where points start to apply) come from an analysis of the
        Home Credit data. The <strong>points</strong> per check were chosen by the team, and checks
        marked † were not analysed against Home Credit.
      </p>
    </section>

    <div class="method-grid">
      <section>
        <h4>Delay <small>Rule 1 · higher = worse</small></h4>
        <p class="method-question">How late does the customer pay, and is it getting worse?</p>
        <ul>
          <li>Share of instalments paid late: up to 30 pts</li>
          <li>Worst days past due on any instalment: up to 20 pts</li>
          <li>Late payments in a row: up to 15 pts</li>
          <li>Average days late when late: up to 10 pts</li>
          <li>Days past due rising over the last 6 months†: up to 10 pts</li>
          <li>Partial payments and unpaid amounts†: up to 10 pts</li>
          <li>Lateness in the last 12 months†: up to 5 pts</li>
        </ul>
        <p class="method-source">From the data: more than 30% late defaults at 12.8–15.8%,
          against 8.07% on average.</p>
      </section>

      <section>
        <h4>Miss pattern <small>Rule 2 · higher = worse</small></h4>
        <p class="method-question">Are the misses a one-off (a holiday, a salary delay) or a
          lasting problem?</p>
        <ul>
          <li>Recovery rate, the share of misses followed by a good payment: up to 30 pts</li>
          <li>Longest streak of misses: up to 25 pts</li>
          <li>How often they miss†: up to 15 pts</li>
          <li>How recent the misses are†: up to 10 pts</li>
          <li>Misses bunched together†: up to 10 pts</li>
          <li>Misses around holidays or month end†: up to −10 pts (lowers the score)</li>
        </ul>
        <p>Below 25 = <strong>Temporary</strong> (a one-off), 25–50 = <strong>Inconclusive</strong>
          (watch it), above 50 = <strong>Sustained</strong> (a lasting problem).</p>
        <p class="method-source">From the data: customers who recover from at least 80% of their
          misses default at 7.84%, slightly below average.</p>
      </section>

      <section>
        <h4>Exposure <small>Rule 3 · higher = worse</small></h4>
        <p class="method-question">How much debt is the customer carrying, and can they afford it?</p>
        <ul>
          <li>Utilisation across loans: up to 25 pts</li>
          <li>Card or overdraft utilisation: up to 20 pts</li>
          <li>Number of active loans: up to 15 pts</li>
          <li>Debt-to-income: up to 15 pts (3 pts if income is unknown)</li>
          <li>New loans taken in the last 3 months†: up to 10 pts</li>
          <li>Outstanding balance rising over time†: up to 10 pts</li>
        </ul>
        <p class="method-source">From the data: card utilisation above 95% defaults at 15.5–20.6%.</p>
      </section>

      <section>
        <h4>Data quality <small>Rule 4 · higher = better · a gate</small></h4>
        <p class="method-question">Do we have enough data to judge this customer at all?</p>
        <ul>
          <li>Number of repayment records: up to 35 pts</li>
          <li>Loan and credit-limit data on file: up to 20 pts</li>
          <li>Months of history: up to 15 pts</li>
          <li>Income and DPD fields present: up to 10 pts</li>
          <li>How recent the data is†: up to 10 pts</li>
          <li>Required fields filled in: up to 10 pts</li>
        </ul>
        <p>Complete ≥ 75 · Adequate 50–74 · Partial 25–49 · Insufficient &lt; 25. It is not added to
          the risk score; it decides whether a score is shown.</p>
        <p class="method-source">From the data: missing data is not a sign of risk. Customers with
          no repayment history defaulted at 5.98%, below average, so a thin file is tagged
          rather than scored low.</p>
      </section>
    </div>

    <section class="method-callout">
      <h4>Insufficient data</h4>
      <p>
        A customer is rated only when data quality is at least 50 <em>and</em> at least 6 months
        of instalments are on file. Below that, the customer is tagged
        <span class="badge badge-insufficient">Insufficient data</span> and the score is blurred,
        because a model trained on full histories cannot judge one or two payments. The risk is
        unknown, not good or bad. The provisional score is still stored for audit.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h4>Models</h4>
      <div class="method-models">
        {models.map((m) => (
          <div class="method-model">
            <div class="method-model-head">
              <strong>{m.name}</strong>
              <span class="badge badge-low">{m.status}</span>
            </div>
            <p>{m.role}</p>
            <dl>
              <dt>Algorithm</dt><dd>{m.algorithm}</dd>
              <dt>Data</dt><dd>{m.data}</dd>
              {m.features && <><dt>Features</dt><dd>{m.features}</dd></>}
              <dt>Quality</dt><dd>{m.quality}</dd>
              <dt>File</dt><dd><code>{m.file}</code></dd>
            </dl>
          </div>
        ))}
      </div>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h4>Where results are stored</h4>
      <p>
        Uploads and assessments are stored in a <strong>SQLite</strong> database
        (<code>backend/data/credx.db</code>, via SQLAlchemy). Each run adds a row to
        <code>warning_assessments</code>, so past scores are kept. Analyst decisions go to
        <code>alert_classifications</code>, together with the evidence at decision time.
      </p>
    </section>
  </div>
</details>
